---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import {AffiliateButton, ComparisonTable, FAQSection, RelatedArticles} from '../../components/article';

const title = 'Best Budget Laptops Under $800 for Remote Work (2025)';
const description = 'Great remote work laptops do not require huge budgets. Tested eight laptops under eight hundred bucks to find the best for productivity, video calls, and battery life.';
const pubDate = new Date('2024-11-24');
const updatedDate = new Date('2024-11-24');
const author = 'FloatJet Team';
const category = 'Computers';
const categorySlug = 'gear';
const readingTime = 14;
const image = 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1525547719571-a2d4ac8945e2?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80';
const imageAlt = 'Budget laptop on desk for remote work';

const headings = [
  {text: 'What Actually Matters', slug: 'what-specs-matter', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Quick Comparison', slug: 'quick-comparison', depth: 2},
  {text: 'The Reviews', slug: 'detailed-reviews', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Acer Aspire 5', slug: 'acer-aspire-5', depth: 3},
  {text: 'Lenovo IdeaPad 5', slug: 'lenovo-ideapad-5', depth: 3},
  {text: 'HP Pavilion 15', slug: 'hp-pavilion-15', depth: 3},
  {text: 'ASUS VivoBook 15', slug: 'asus-vivobook-15', depth: 3},
  {text: 'Dell Inspiron 15', slug: 'dell-inspiron-15', depth: 3},
  {text: 'Lenovo ThinkPad E-Series', slug: 'thinkpad-e-series', depth: 3},
  {text: 'Acer Swift 3', slug: 'acer-swift-3', depth: 3},
  {text: 'HP 15', slug: 'hp-15', depth: 3},
  {text: 'Windows vs Chromebook', slug: 'windows-vs-chromebook', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Finding Deals', slug: 'best-deals', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Refurbished Guide', slug: 'refurbished-guide', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Questions', slug: 'faq', depth: 2},
];

const comparisonColumns = [
  {key: 'laptop', label: 'Laptop', width: '140px'},
  {key: 'processor', label: 'Processor'},
  {key: 'ram', label: 'RAM'},
  {key: 'battery', label: 'Battery'},
  {key: 'price', label: 'Price'},
];

const comparisonRows = [
  {laptop: '<strong>Acer Aspire 5</strong>', processor: 'i5-1235U', ram: '8GB', battery: '10 hrs', price: '$550'},
  {
    laptop: '<strong>Lenovo IdeaPad 5</strong>',
    processor: 'Ryzen 5 5500U',
    ram: '8GB',
    battery: '10 hrs',
    price: '$600'
  },
  {laptop: '<strong>HP Pavilion 15</strong>', processor: 'i5-1235U', ram: '8GB', battery: '8 hrs', price: '$650'},
  {
    laptop: '<strong>ASUS VivoBook 15</strong>',
    processor: 'Ryzen 5 5500U',
    ram: '8GB',
    battery: '8 hrs',
    price: '$500'
  },
  {laptop: '<strong>Dell Inspiron 15</strong>', processor: 'i5-1235U', ram: '8GB', battery: '8 hrs', price: '$580'},
  {laptop: '<strong>ThinkPad E14</strong>', processor: 'i5-1235U', ram: '8GB', battery: '10 hrs', price: '$750'},
  {laptop: '<strong>Acer Swift 3</strong>', processor: 'i5-1240P', ram: '8GB', battery: '11 hrs', price: '$700'},
  {laptop: '<strong>HP 15</strong>', processor: 'i3-1215U', ram: '8GB', battery: '7 hrs', price: '$400'},
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Eight gig RAM enough?',
    answer: 'Video calls, docs, browsing—plenty. Slack plus Zoom plus Chrome tabs? Handles it. Video editing or real multitasking? Get sixteen.',
  },
  {
    question: 'SSD or HDD?',
    answer: 'SSD. Windows boots in ten seconds vs two minutes. Two fifty-six SSD beats one terabyte HDD. Always.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Handle video calls?',
    answer: 'All of them. Zoom, Teams, Meet—fine. Webcams vary quality-wise. Some grainy. 720p works though.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How long do they last?',
    answer: 'Three to five years usually. ThinkPads and Dells survive longer. Take care of the battery and keep it cool.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Refurbished worth it?',
    answer: 'From Dell Outlet or Lenovo Outlet? Yeah. Thirty to forty percent off with warranty. Random marketplace sellers? Nope.',
  },
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Best Laptops for Remote Work',
    excerpt: 'Our complete guide including premium options.',
    href: '/gear/best-laptops-remote-work',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1496181133206-80ce9b88a853?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Premium laptop on desk',
  },
  {
    title: 'MacBook vs Windows for Remote Work',
    excerpt: 'Which platform is right for your work style?',
    href: '/gear/macbook-vs-windows-remote-work',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1517336714731-489689fd1ca8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'MacBook and Windows laptop comparison',
  },
  {
    title: 'How Much RAM Do You Need?',
    excerpt: 'Find the right amount of memory for your workflow.',
    href: '/gear/how-much-ram-remote-work',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1518770660439-4636190af475?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Computer RAM memory',
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    updatedDate={updatedDate}
    author={author}
    category={category}
    categorySlug={categorySlug}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Spent a month helping my cousin find a work laptop. His budget was six hundred bucks. Everyone kept telling him to
    just save more for a MacBook. But he needed something now. Turns out there are solid options under eight hundred.
    Eight laptops tested. Here's what actually worked.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-specs-matter">What Actually Matters</h2>

  <p>
    Gaming benchmarks? Useless. Here's what actually matters when you're working remote:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Processor.</strong> i5 or Ryzen 5. Either works. Stop worrying about it.</li>
    <li><strong>RAM.</strong> Eight gig minimum. Sixteen if you keep lots of tabs open or use heavy apps.</li>
    <li><strong>Storage.</strong> SSD only. Two fifty-six gig minimum, five twelve preferred. No spinning hard drives in
      2025.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Display.</strong> 1080p with IPS. The viewing angles matter when you're adjusting screen position.</li>
    <li><strong>Battery.</strong> Eight hours or more. Less and you're tethered to outlets.</li>
    <li><strong>Webcam.</strong> 720p is okay. 1080p is better. Most budget ones are just okay.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyboard.</strong> You'll type on it all day. Test it if you can.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>What to skip: Dedicated graphics cards. 4K screens. High refresh rates. None of that helps spreadsheets or video
    calls. Just costs more.</p>

  <h2 id="quick-comparison">Quick Comparison</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={comparisonColumns} rows={comparisonRows}/>

  <h2 id="detailed-reviews">The Reviews</h2>

  <h3 id="acer-aspire-5">Acer Aspire 5</h3>

  <p>
    My cousin's laptop now. Five fifty gets you a current-gen Intel, usable screen, and—here's the kicker—you can
    actually upgrade the RAM and storage later. Most budget machines solder everything down.
  </p>

  <p>
    Running an i5-1235U with eight gigs of RAM. Five twelve gig SSD. Screen is fifteen point six inches at 1080p IPS.
    Battery lasts roughly ten hours. Weight sits at three point nine pounds.
  </p>

  <p>All plastic construction. Looks cheap. Functions fine. My cousin's been using it for five months now without
    complaints.</p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/acer-aspire-5" text="Check Acer Aspire 5 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="lenovo-ideapad-5">Lenovo IdeaPad 5</h3>

  <p>
    Brighter screen. Three hundred nits vs the usual two fifty on budget machines. Actually readable in sunny cafes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ryzen 5 5500U inside. Eight gig DDR4. Five twelve SSD. Fifteen point six inch screen at 1080p IPS. Gets about ten
    hours. Three point seven pounds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Runs multiple Chrome windows, Slack, Zoom, random tabs—smooth enough. Bezels are thin so the screen feels roomier
    than the spec suggests.
  </p>

  <p>Six hundred. Fifty more than the Aspire for a better display. Fair trade if your workspace is bright.</p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/lenovo-ideapad-5" text="Check Lenovo IdeaPad 5 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="hp-pavilion-15">HP Pavilion 15</h3>

  <p>
    Metal lid. Metal palm rest. Doesn't creak when you pick it up. Definitely looks more expensive than six fifty.
  </p>

  <p>
    i5-1235U. Eight gig RAM. Five twelve SSD. Fifteen point six inch 1080p IPS. About eight hours battery. Three point
    eight pounds.
  </p>

  <p>
    The speakers are B&O tuned. They're actually... not terrible? Video calls sound cleaner than most. Nice if you
    forget headphones.
  </p>

  <p>Shorter battery than Lenovo or Acer. That's the compromise for the nicer build.</p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/hp-pavilion-15" text="Check HP Pavilion 15 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="asus-vivobook-15">ASUS VivoBook 15</h3>

  <p>
    Lightest fifteen-incher here. Three point five pounds. The ErgoLift hinge is neat—props the keyboard at an angle
    when you open it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ryzen 5 5500U. Eight gig DDR4. Five twelve SSD. Fifteen point six inch 1080p screen. Eight hours battery.
  </p>

  <p>
    Fingerprint reader built into the power button. You'll unlock this thing constantly. That little sensor saves time.
  </p>

  <p>Five hundred. Light and cheap? This is your answer.</p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/asus-vivobook-15" text="Check ASUS VivoBook 15 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="dell-inspiron-15">Dell Inspiron 15</h3>

  <p>
    The Toyota Camry of laptops. Boring. Predictable. Absolutely everywhere.
  </p>

  <p>
    i5-1235U. Eight gig DDR4. Five twelve SSD. Fifteen point six at 1080p. Eight hours battery. Three point nine pounds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Something breaks? Dell service centers exist in most cities. Parts are easy to find. Repairs don't require shipping
    to some mystery warehouse.
  </p>

  <p>Five eighty. Mid-range price, mid-range everything. Sometimes boring is good.</p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/dell-inspiron-15" text="Check Dell Inspiron 15 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="thinkpad-e-series">Lenovo ThinkPad E14</h3>

  <p>
    That keyboard. The one ThinkPad fans talk about constantly. Typed on one for a week. They're right.
  </p>

  <p>
    i5-1235U. Eight gig DDR4, upgradeable later. Two fifty-six SSD which is tight but also upgradeable. Fourteen inch
    1080p IPS. Ten hours battery. Three point five pounds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Write all day? Emails, docs, code? This keyboard makes a difference. The E-series is ThinkPad on a budget. Seven
    fifty puts it at the top of this guide but the typing alone justifies it for some people.
  </p>

  <p>MIL-SPEC rated. Designed to survive getting dropped. Storage is cramped but swappable.</p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/thinkpad-e14" text="Check ThinkPad E14 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="acer-swift-3">Acer Swift 3</h3>

  <p>
    Under three pounds. Eleven hour battery. Aluminum construction. Basically an ultrabook that snuck into the budget
    category.
  </p>

  <p>
    i5-1240P. Eight gig LPDDR4X. Five twelve SSD. Fourteen inch 1080p IPS. Two point six pounds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Fourteen inches hits a sweet spot for travel. Big enough to work on, small enough to not dominate your bag. Battery
    survives full workdays.
  </p>

  <p>Seven hundred. High end of budget territory. But true portability costs money.</p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/acer-swift-3" text="Check Acer Swift 3 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3 id="hp-15">HP 15</h3>

  <p>
    Four hundred dollars. That's the whole pitch.
  </p>

  <p>
    i3-1215U instead of i5. Only two fifty-six SSD. Battery dies at seven hours. But Zoom runs. Docs run. Email runs.
  </p>

  <p>
    Eight gig DDR4. Fifteen point six inch 1080p screen. Three point seven pounds.
  </p>

  <p>Light usage? Backup machine? Secondary device? It works. Just don't expect speed. Slower than everything else here.
    But it runs.</p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/hp-15" text="Check HP 15 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h2 id="windows-vs-chromebook">Windows vs Chromebook</h2>

  <h3>Windows If:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You need actual Office apps, not just the web versions</li>
    <li>Your job requires Windows-only software</li>
    <li>You work offline regularly</li>
    <li>You want to install whatever you want</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Chromebook If:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Everything you do is in a browser anyway</li>
    <li>Google Workspace is your whole life</li>
    <li>You hate Windows updates and maintenance</li>
    <li>Budget is super tight (two to four hundred bucks)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Most remote workers need Windows. Chromebooks work for specific situations but limit what you can do.</p>

  <h2 id="best-deals">Finding Deals</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Prime Day (July):</strong> Decent laptop discounts. Worth waiting if timing works.</li>
    <li><strong>Black Friday:</strong> Best prices all year. Plan around this.</li>
    <li><strong>August/September:</strong> Back to school sales. Work for anyone.</li>
    <li><strong>Dell Outlet:</strong> Refurbs with real warranties.</li>
    <li><strong>Lenovo Sales:</strong> Happens monthly. Forty percent off sometimes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="refurbished-guide">Buying Refurbished</h2>

  <p>
    Refurbished saves thirty to fifty percent. But buy smart.
  </p>

  <h3>Good Places</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dell Outlet:</strong> Factory refurbs, real warranty included</li>
    <li><strong>Lenovo Outlet:</strong> Same idea</li>
    <li><strong>Amazon Renewed:</strong> Their guarantee backs it</li>
    <li><strong>Best Buy Open Box:</strong> Just returned, usually fine</li>
    <li><strong>Back Market:</strong> Grades condition clearly</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Check For</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ninety days warranty minimum. One year preferred.</li>
    <li>Clear grading (A, B, C condition)</li>
    <li>Battery health info</li>
    <li>Real return policy</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqItems}/>

  <h2>Bottom Line</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Aspire 5</strong> for most people. Upgradeable. Reliable. Done.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>ThinkPad E14</strong> if typing matters. That keyboard is genuinely better.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Swift 3</strong> if you travel. Under three pounds. All-day battery.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>HP 15</strong> if money's tight. Four hundred and it works.
  </p>

  <p>You don't need a thousand-dollar machine. Grab one. Start working.</p>

  <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
</ArticleLayout>
